Falsifies `kernel::arch::riscv64::iommu::proofs::no_device_can_reach_another_devices_context`.

The defect is a bound written for one device-context format and run under the other. The
base format is 32 bytes and 128 of them fill a frame; the extended format that QEMU's
`riscv-iommu-pci` actually selects (it reports `CAPS.MSI_FLAT`) is 64 bytes, and only 64
fit. Writing the bound as `FRAME_SIZE / 32`, from the spec's base-format table or from a
reading of the directory as "one frame of 128 entries", admits requester ids 64 through
127 at a 64-byte stride, and their contexts are written past the end of the directory's
single frame into whatever the allocator handed out next.

**Every test in this tree stays green under it**, reasoned rather than run: the one PCIe
disk on `virt` has a requester id far below 64, so nothing ever addresses a context past
the frame. It is the same shape as the SMMU's `STRTAB_LOG2` falsification one
architecture over, with the stride chosen at run time from a capability bit rather than
fixed at compile time.

Expected to fail: `assert!(oa + dc_bytes <= page_frames::FRAME_SIZE, "every context the
bound admits lies inside the directory's single frame")`. Confirmed red on 2026-09-25
(UTC), on an aarch64 host.

The sibling harness `the_iommu_is_handed_exactly_the_domain_the_kernel_built` stays green
under this patch: it builds a context's words and never places one.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/iommu.rs b/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/iommu.rs
index 1fa25c3f7..5e5c95cbc 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/iommu.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/iommu.rs
@@ -180,7 +180,7 @@ fn queue_base(pa: u64, log2: u32) -> u64 {
 /// function of its own: `no_device_can_reach_another_devices_context` assumes exactly what it
 /// admits and proves the stride in `context_offset` agrees with it.
 fn is_in_directory(rid: u32, dc_bytes: u64) -> bool {
-    (rid as u64) < page_frames::FRAME_SIZE / dc_bytes
+    (rid as u64) < page_frames::FRAME_SIZE / 32
 }
 
 /// Where device `rid`'s context sits in the device directory, as a byte offset into its frame.
